blisher": "FinOps Foundation"}, {"title": "FOCUS Specification v1.3 (PDF)", "url": "https://focus.finops.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/FOCUS_spec-v1_3.pdf", "publisher": "FinOps Foundation", "date": "Dec 2025"}, {"title": "FinOps Foundation extends specification to rein in cloud costs in the AI era", "url": "https://techstrong.it/featured/finops-foundation-extends-specification-to-rein-in-cloud-costs-in-the-ai-era/", "publisher": "Techstrong IT", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "FOCUS specification and AI cost accountability at FinOps X", "url": "https://siliconangle.com/2026/06/08/focus-specification-ai-cost-accountability-finopsx/", "publisher": "SiliconANGLE", "date": "8 Jun 2026"}, {"title": "FOCUS spec, Kubernetes and AI costs", "url": "https://cast.ai/blog/focus-spec-kubernetes-ai-costs/", "publisher": "Cast AI"}]}-->
<p>A typical AI cost conversation: finance sees one large invoice from a model provider, another from a GPU cloud, a growing line for AI features in SaaS tools, and a Kubernetes bill that now includes GPU nodes. Engineering can't say which product or customer drove any of it. Without that, AI unit economics are guesswork.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What FOCUS is</h2>
<p>FOCUS, the FinOps Open Cost and Usage Specification, is the FinOps Foundation's open standard for billing data. The idea is simple: if every provider publishes cost and usage in the same columns, you can combine cloud, SaaS and AI spend in one dataset without writing a custom parser for each.</p>
<p>The standard has moved quickly. Version 1.3 added split cost allocation and a contract commitments dataset<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. The FinOps Foundation reports that its steering committee ratified version 1.4 on 4 June 2026, adding new datasets and columns<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>, and coverage from FinOps X described additional data integrity tooling<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Where AI fits, and where it doesn't yet</h2>
<p>AI usage can be represented with existing columns: token counts as a consumed quantity and unit, the model and price point as a SKU, and AI services under a service category<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. That is enough to put model API spend next to everything else.</p>
<p>It is not yet enough for detailed token economics. Reports around FinOps X 2026 described plans for version 1.5 to break down AI spend by token type and workload<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Until that lands and providers adopt it, input versus output tokens, cached tokens and per-feature attribution mostly have to come from your own telemetry.</p>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Cost source</th><th>Where the data comes from today</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Hyperscaler AI services</td><td>Provider billing exports, increasingly in FOCUS format</td></tr>
<tr><td>Model API providers</td><td>Invoices and usage APIs; detail varies by provider</td></tr>
<tr><td>Self-hosted models on Kubernetes</td><td>Cluster cost allocation, with GPU attribution per workload<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>AI features in SaaS tools</td><td>Seat and add-on licences, often outside any usage feed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Per-feature and per-customer usage</td><td>Your own gateway and tracing telemetry</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The architecture I use</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>A model gateway for every call</strong>, which records model, input and output tokens, latency and the calling team, product, feature and, where relevant, customer.</li>
<li><strong>Tracing with GenAI attributes</strong>, as in <a href="/insights/llm-observability-opentelemetry-genai/">LLM observability with OpenTelemetry</a>, so cost can be tied to user journeys rather than just API keys.</li>
<li><strong>FOCUS as the finance format.</strong> Normalise provider exports to FOCUS, and publish internal usage in compatible columns so finance sees one dataset.</li>
<li><strong>GPU allocation for self-hosted models</strong>, using the cluster techniques in <a href="/insights/kubernetes-finops-scale-to-zero/">Kubernetes FinOps</a> and <a href="/insights/kubernetes-dra-gpu-scheduling/">DRA for GPUs</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Reconciliation each month</strong> between gateway totals and invoices. Gaps reveal untracked keys and shadow usage.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p>The single most useful number is cost per successful outcome: per resolved ticket, per document processed, per qualified answer. Tokens are an input; outcomes are what the business buys.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Decisions this unlocks</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Model routing.</strong> Send simple requests to cheaper models once you can see which features use expensive ones.</li>
<li><strong>Build versus buy.</strong> Compare API spend with the cost of self-hosting, as in <a href="/insights/build-vs-buy-ai-capabilities/">build vs buy for AI</a>, using real usage instead of estimates.</li>
<li><strong>Pricing.</strong> Set customer pricing for AI features with actual unit costs, not hopes.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I can help</h2>
<p>I set up AI cost visibility and unit economics, from gateways and tagging to FOCUS-based reporting finance can use. As a <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> I can own the cost roadmap alongside the technology one. Or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
